### Why does RSocket clash with serverless?

The mismatch has three roots. Serverless functions are stateless and ephemeral; they cannot hold a connection open for a streaming session that lasts minutes. Cold starts add latency when a new function instance spins up. Function timeouts bound how long any single invocation can run, ruling out indefinite streams.

```text
RSocket needs:                     Serverless provides:
  Long-lived connections             Ephemeral functions (seconds)
  Stateful streams                   Stateless invocations
  Resume tokens                      Scale-to-zero (no state)
  Backpressure over time             Cold starts + timeouts
```

### What is Pattern 1: always-on server with serverless compute?

An always-on RSocket server (a container on ECS, Kubernetes, or a VM) owns persistent connections and delegates stateless compute to serverless functions.

```java
package com.bank.rsocket;

import io.rsocket.Payload;
import io.rsocket.RSocket;
import io.rsocket.util.DefaultPayload;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.lambda.LambdaClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.lambda.model.InvokeRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.core.SdkBytes;

public class AlwaysOnServer implements RSocket {
    private final LambdaClient lambda = LambdaClient.create();

    @Override
    public Mono<Payload> requestResponse(Payload payload) {
        String accountId = payload.getDataUtf8();

        // Delegate the stateless compute to a Lambda function
        return Mono.fromCallable(() -> {
            InvokeRequest request = InvokeRequest.builder()
                    .functionName("calculate-balance")
                    .payload(SdkBytes.fromUtf8String(
                            "{\"accountId\":\"" + accountId + "\"}"))
                    .build();
            String result = lambda.invoke(request)
                    .payload().asUtf8String();
            return DefaultPayload.create(result);
        });
    }
}
```

```text
Architecture:
  Rajesh's app ──RSocket──▶ Always-on server ──Lambda──▶ Calculate
                 (persistent)     (delegates)     (stateless)
```

### What is Pattern 2: API Gateway WebSocket proxy?

API Gateway maintains the WebSocket connection on behalf of the backend and invokes a function per incoming message. The socket lifetime is decoupled from the function lifetime.

```java
package com.bank.serverless;

import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.Context;
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.RequestHandler;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

public class WebSocketMessageHandler
        implements RequestHandler<Map<String, Object>, Map<String, Object>> {

    @Override
    public Map<String, Object> handleRequest(
            Map<String, Object> event, Context context) {
        // API Gateway holds the WebSocket
        // This function handles one message
        String body = (String) event.get("body");
        String connectionId = (String) ((Map<?, ?>) event
                .get("requestContext")).get("connectionId");

        BigDecimal balance = lookupBalance(body);

        // Post response back through API Gateway
        return Map.of(
                "statusCode", 200,
                "body", "{\"balance\":" + balance + "}"
        );
    }

    private BigDecimal lookupBalance(String accountId) {
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }
}
```

### What is Pattern 3: serverless function as RSocket client?

A serverless function acts as a short-lived RSocket client making a request-response call to an always-on RSocket server within its timeout.

```java
package com.bank.serverless;

import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.Context;
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.RequestHandler;
import io.rsocket.core.RSocketConnector;
import io.rsocket.transport.netty.client.TcpClientTransport;
import io.rsocket.util.DefaultPayload;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RSocketClientFunction
        implements RequestHandler<String, String> {

    @Override
    public String handleRequest(String accountId, Context context) {
        // Short-lived RSocket client: connect, call, disconnect
        String result = RSocketConnector.create()
                .connect(TcpClientTransport.create(
                        "rsocket-server.bank.internal", 7000))
                .flatMap(rSocket ->
                        rSocket.requestResponse(
                                DefaultPayload.create(accountId))
                                .map(p -> p.getDataUtf8())
                                .doFinally(s -> rSocket.dispose()))
                .block();

        return "{\"balance\":" + result + "}";
    }
}
```

### Which interaction models fit serverless?

Request-response and fire-and-forget can fit short-lived functions. Request-stream and request-channel need a durable connection that serverless does not provide.

| Model | Serverless Compatible | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Request-response | Yes | Short, bounded, fits within timeout |
| Fire-and-forget | Yes | No response needed |
| Request-stream | No | Needs a long-lived connection |
| Request-channel | No | Needs a persistent bidirectional connection |

```java
// Fits serverless: request-response within timeout
rSocket.requestResponse(DefaultPayload.create("SAV2001"))
        .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(25))  // Lambda timeout minus margin
        .block();

// Does NOT fit serverless: indefinite stream
// rSocket.requestStream(DefaultPayload.create("RELIANCE"))
//         .subscribe();  // function will be killed before stream ends
```

### What about connection pooling in serverless?

Functions cannot maintain connection pools across invocations reliably. Repeatedly opening RSocket connections adds setup cost and can exhaust server connection limits.

```java
// WRONG: opening a new RSocket connection per invocation
public String handleRequest(String input, Context context) {
    // Every cold start opens a new TCP connection
    // Warm invocations might reuse, but no guarantee
    RSocket rSocket = RSocketConnector.create()
            .connect(TcpClientTransport.create("server", 7000))
            .block();
    // ...
}

// BETTER: use a static field to reuse across warm invocations
private static RSocket rSocket;

static {
    rSocket = RSocketConnector.create()
            .connect(TcpClientTransport.create("server", 7000))
            .block();
}
```

### What are common mistakes with RSocket and serverless?

The most common mistake is trying to run a long-lived stream inside a serverless function.

```java
// WRONG: streaming inside a Lambda function
public String handleRequest(String input, Context context) {
    rSocket.requestStream(DefaultPayload.create("RELIANCE"))
            .subscribe(p -> process(p));
    // Function times out and is killed, stream is lost
    return "started"; // misleading
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Put durable, stateful connectivity on the always-on tier and reserve serverless for stateless, bounded units of work. At National Bank of India Rajesh's live price stream lives on the always-on edge because no function can hold it, while a one-off balance calculation is a perfect stateless job that the edge delegates to a serverless function.

- Backpressure and resumption are meaningless if the endpoint vanishes mid-stream.
- Translate at the boundary: RSocket terminates on the persistent edge, and beyond that use queues and function triggers.
- Account for connection setup cost when using functions as RSocket clients.
